\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSFontPanel class implements the Font Panel\'d0a user-interface object that displays a list of available fonts, letting the user preview them and change the font used to display text. The actual changes are made through conversion messages sent to the shared NSFontManager instance. There\'27s only one Font Panel for each application.\
\fs14\
\fs28 In general, you add the facilities of the Font Panel to your application, along with the NSFontManager and the Font menu, through Interface Builder. You do this by dragging a Font menu into one of your application\'27s menus. At run time, when the user chooses the Font Panel command for the first time, the Font Panel object is created and hooked into the font conversion system. You can also create (or access) the Font Panel using the \b sharedFontPanel\b0  class method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 You can add a custom view object to an NSFontPanel using \b setAccessoryView:\b0 , or limit the fonts display by assigning a delegate to the application\'27s font manager object. If you want the NSFontManager to instantiate the Font Panel from some class other than NSFontPanel, use NSFontManager\'27s \b setFontPanelFactory:\b0  class method. See the NSFontManager class specification for more information on using the font conversion system.\
\fs14\
